\section{Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) and resistivity}

On each of the three crystals the transition-metal NMR remains a single line over the temperature range of the measurements below. Figure~\ref{fig:local} shows the spin-lattice relaxation rate divided by temperature, \(1/(T_1 T)\), versus inverse temperature. Figure~\ref{fig:transport} shows the in-plane resistivity. Each curve in Figures~\ref{fig:local} and~\ref{fig:transport} is normalized to its value at \(T=4D\). Below the temperature range of Figure~\ref{fig:transport} the resistance of the \(\nu=1/2\) crystal exceeds the range of the instrument; it keeps rising down to \(T=0.05D\), and no diffraction peak appears down to that temperature.

