\section{Task}

Use the measurement data in \texttt{measurements.json} as given, rather than data published for a named compound. Give energies in units of \(D\).

\begin{enumerate}
\item Propose the effective theory you find most credible. State the degrees of freedom, the lattice and the unit cell, and which symmetries the model has and which it lacks. Write out every term in the Hamiltonian. You may use a distinct symbol to denote the coefficient of each term. Give the value of each coefficient and an estimate of its uncertainty, both determined from the available measurement data.

\item Draw the phase diagram of this Hamiltonian. The horizontal axis is the filling fraction \(\nu\), from 0 to 1. The vertical axis is the temperature. Mark the three crystals by vertical lines at \(\nu=1/2\), \(\nu\approx 0.3\), and \(\nu\approx 0.2\). For each crystal, state the phases from low temperature to high temperature.

\item Use this effective theory to explain the diffraction, the STS differential conductance, the NMR spin-lattice relaxation rate, and the resistivity. For each set of measurement data, state which feature of the theory produces it.

\item Predict the low-temperature angle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy (ARPES) intensity at \(\nu=1/2\), at \(\nu\approx 0.3\), and at \(\nu\approx 0.2\), after division by the Fermi function. For each filling, draw a density plot of this intensity along \((0,0)\) to \((1/2,0)\), then to \((1/2,1/2)\), and back to \((0,0)\), with momentum on the horizontal axis and energy on the vertical axis. Choose the energy window. Compare the three fillings. Name the features of physical significance, and state what each implies. State the momentum assignment you use for the band minimum.

\item From your predicted spectrum at \(\nu=1/2\), recover the Green's function by the Kramers–Kronig relation. Measure \(\omega\) from the chemical potential of this crystal in the low-temperature limit; that chemical potential lies at the midpoint of the gap. Inside the gap the Green's function is real and has a surface of zeros \(\omega_0(k)\). Plot \(\omega_0(k)\) along the same path as in task 4, and state where it lies in the gap, in which direction it disperses relative to the two bands, and by how much. Then, for a reference energy \(\omega_r\) swept across the gap, plot the fraction of the Brillouin zone on which \(\operatorname{Re} G(k,\omega_r)>0\), mark the value at \(\omega_r=0\), and compare that value with \(\nu\).
\end{enumerate}

A workstation-scale calculation is enough when you use one. State the approximation and the system size.

